Here's a Java solution to determine if an integer `n` is a power of three without using loops or recursion:

```java
class PowerOfThree {
    public static boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // Check for negative values
        if (n < 0) {
            return false;
        }
        // Check if n is an integer power of 3
        return (Math.log(n) / Math.log(3)) % 1 == 0;
    }
}
```

This solution works because a power of three can be represented as `3^x`, where `x` is an integer. The presence of a fractional part in `(log n / log 3)` indicates that `n` is not an integer power of three, as the log function yields a non-integer result if the base and exponent are not integer powers of each other. In contrast, for integer powers, the fractional part after the division will be 0. The `% 1` operator extracts and discards the fractional part of the result, and if it is non-zero, the function returns false, otherwise true. This approach is efficient and avoids using loops or recursion.

